{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.40.5.42", "inferred_role": "compromised_internal_application_server_audit_tamper_target", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:38:18+0200", "last_seen": "2027-09-29T02:48:42+0200", "agent_name": "APP-SRV-03"},
    {"ip": "10.40.2.10", "inferred_role": "internal_dc_pivot_origin_of_rdp_session", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:38:18+0200", "last_seen": "2027-09-29T02:38:18+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Lateral Movement (RDP from DC)", "technique": "T1078 + T1021.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-29 02:38:18 CEST, Windows Security Event 4624 'An account was successfully logged on' fired on APP-SRV-03 (10.40.5.42) with targetUserName=svc-admin, logonType=10 (RemoteInteractive = RDP), ipAddress=10.40.2.10 (a domain controller). A service account logging in INTERACTIVELY via RDP from a DC at 02:38 in the morning is anomalous on three independent axes: (a) service accounts should never produce logonType=10, (b) RDP originating FROM a DC towards a member server is a backward propagation pattern typical of post-compromise pivoting, (c) 02:38 is outside business hours. The session is the entry point for the audit-tampering activity that follows.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 on agent.name=APP-SRV-03 with data.win.eventdata.targetUserName=svc-admin + logonType=10 + ipAddress=10.40.2.10. Service-account + interactive-logon + DC-as-source = compromised-service-account-via-DC-pivot signature.", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:38:18+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Impair Defenses (Disable Audit Subcategory — Logon)", "technique": "T1562.002", "what_happened": "At 02:42:14 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on APP-SRV-03: image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\auditpol.exe, commandLine='auditpol.exe /set /subcategory:\"Logon\" /success:disable /failure:disable', parentImage=C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe, user=CORP\\svc-admin. The attacker is silencing future Windows Security Event 4624/4625/4634/4647 (Logon subcategory) audit generation — meaning subsequent authentication activity on this host will NOT be recorded going forward. This is the silent-telemetry-kill modality of T1562.002 (vs T1070.001 which wipes existing logs): the attacker turns off the recording rather than erasing the tape, making everything they do afterwards INVISIBLE to defenders.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: data.win.eventdata.image ending with auditpol.exe + commandLine matching '/set /subcategory:\"Logon\"' AND '/success:disable' + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=CORP\\svc-admin on agent.name=APP-SRV-03. auditpol.exe disabling the Logon subcategory is a high-confidence T1562.002 indicator.", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:42:14+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Impair Defenses (Disable Audit Subcategory — Process Creation)", "technique": "T1562.002", "what_happened": "At 02:42:18 CEST (4 seconds later), Sysmon Event 1 fired on APP-SRV-03: image=auditpol.exe, commandLine='auditpol.exe /set /subcategory:\"Process Creation\" /success:disable', parentImage=cmd.exe, user=CORP\\svc-admin. The attacker now silences Windows Security Event 4688 'A new process has been created' — future process executions on this host will NOT produce 4688. Note: Sysmon Event 1 continues to fire independently (Sysmon is a separate kernel-mode telemetry source not controlled by Windows audit policy), which is why we still see this event; the attacker may not realize this distinction. Disabling Process Creation auditing is the second sub-step of a systematic audit kill.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=auditpol.exe + commandLine matching '/set /subcategory:\"Process Creation\"' AND '/success:disable'. The 4-second gap to the previous auditpol command is consistent with scripted execution (no human types two auditpol commands 4 seconds apart by hand).", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:42:18+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Impair Defenses (Disable Audit Subcategory — File System)", "technique": "T1562.002", "what_happened": "At 02:42:22 CEST (4 seconds later), Sysmon Event 1 fired on APP-SRV-03: image=auditpol.exe, commandLine='auditpol.exe /set /subcategory:\"File System\" /success:disable', parentImage=cmd.exe, user=CORP\\svc-admin. The attacker now silences Windows Security Event 4663 'An attempt was made to access an object' for File System — future file access on this host will NOT be recorded. Combined with steps 2-3, the attacker has now created a TELEMETRY-BLIND WINDOW: Logon auth, process creation, and file access are all NOT being captured by the Windows Security log. The systematic 4-second-interval sequence (Logon → Process Creation → File System) is the audit-kill universal pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=auditpol.exe + commandLine matching '/set /subcategory:\"File System\"' AND '/success:disable'. Three auditpol disables in 8 seconds total = systematic-audit-kill signature (T1562.002, multi-subcategory variant).", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:42:22+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Impair Defenses (Audit Policy Change Detected)", "technique": "T1562.002", "what_happened": "At 02:46:11 CEST, Windows Security Event 4719 'System audit policy was changed' fired on APP-SRV-03 with SubjectUserName=svc-admin, SubcategoryGUID={0CCE9215-69AE-11D9-BED3-505054503030} (Logon subcategory GUID), AuditPolicyChanges='%%8448 %%8450' (decoded: %%8448=Success removed, %%8450=Failure removed). Event 4719 is the BUILT-IN audit-policy-change signal — Windows fires it even when the attacker disables the subcategory that records it (the meta-policy event survives because it is generated by the LSA security subsystem, not by the audit subcategory itself). This is the canonical T1562.002 detection event: if 4719 fires with AuditPolicyChanges containing %%8448 or %%8450 outside an approved change window, it is by definition audit tampering. The ~4-minute gap between the auditpol commands at 02:42:14-22 and the 4719 at 02:46:11 reflects Windows' batched audit-policy event emission (policy changes are aggregated by LSA before the 4719 fires).", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4719 on agent.name=APP-SRV-03 with data.win.eventdata.subjectUserName=svc-admin. The SubcategoryGUID {0CCE9215-69AE-11D9-BED3-505054503030} corresponds to the Logon subcategory; AuditPolicyChanges field containing '%%8448' (Success removed) or '%%8450' (Failure removed) = audit-policy-change-with-removal = T1562.002 indicator.", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:46:11+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "SIEM Visibility Degradation Detected", "technique": "T1562.002 effect", "what_happened": "At 02:46:14 CEST (3 seconds after the 4719), Wazuh SIEM Health Monitoring fired alert LOG_VOLUME_DROP for host=APP-SRV-03, source=WindowsSecurity, count=0 events/min (vs baseline 230 events/min over the past 7 days). The log volume on APP-SRV-03 collapsed to ZERO immediately after the audit-policy change — concrete confirmation that the attacker's intent succeeded: this host is now telemetry-blind to the SIEM for the Windows Security channel. This SIEM-Health-derived signal is the second-stage detection layer for T1562.002 — even if 4719 had been missed somehow, the log-volume-drop alarm catches the effect.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule: SIEM health monitoring log-volume-drop signature, rule.id 100502 (custom), level 13. Trigger condition: agent.name X log-volume of source=WindowsSecurity drops by >90% from baseline within rolling 5-minute window. Direct causal pairing with the 4719 audit-policy change 3 seconds earlier.", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:46:14+0200"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Defense Evasion (Audit Buffer Wipe — Existing Logs Cleared)", "technique": "T1070.001", "what_happened": "At 02:48:08 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on APP-SRV-03: image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\wevtutil.exe, commandLine='wevtutil.exe cl Security', parentImage=cmd.exe, user=CORP\\svc-admin. After STOPPING future log generation (steps 2-4 = T1562.002), the attacker now WIPES existing log buffer with wevtutil cl Security (= clear Security event log). This is T1070.001 (Clear Windows Event Logs) and removes the historical record of steps 1-5 from the Security channel — the attacker is removing forensic evidence of the audit-disable activity itself. At 02:48:42, Windows Security Event 1102 'The audit log was cleared' fires (the only event Windows ALWAYS emits at clear time, by design — Microsoft's anti-tampering measure). The combination of T1562.002 (stop recording) + T1070.001 (wipe existing) is the FULL audit-kill ritual: defenders see neither future activity nor past activity.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=wevtutil.exe + commandLine matching 'cl Security' + user=CORP\\svc-admin. Windows Security Event 1102 with subjectUserName=svc-admin on the same agent.name. Sysmon witnesses the clear command, Windows witnesses the clear-fact-itself.", "first_seen": "2027-09-29T02:48:08+0200"}
  ],
